WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.299 --> 00:00:02.029
Chatbots reward expertise.

2
00:00:03.338 --> 00:00:06.197
For many tasks, the knowledge
is already in the chatbot.

3
00:00:06.302 --> 00:00:08.087
The hard part is getting it out.

4
00:00:08.650 --> 00:00:11.472
The questions you ask,
the specialist terms you use,

5
00:00:11.512 --> 00:00:15.154
and the suggestions you make
all steer it towards a particular area

6
00:00:15.194 --> 00:00:17.764
of its knowledge, its semantic space.

7
00:00:18.256 --> 00:00:22.679
As programmer Sean Goedecke puts it,
LLMs reward expertise.

8
00:00:22.724 --> 00:00:26.966
A novice and an expert doing the same task
get very different results.

9
00:00:27.387 --> 00:00:31.225
Uploading a relevant document is
a shortcut to steering the chatbot

10
00:00:31.265 --> 00:00:32.465
towards expert answers.

11
00:00:32.916 --> 00:00:35.860
Chatbots work by predicting
the next word in a sequence

12
00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:39.590
that includes the whole chat so far
and any documents you’ve uploaded.

13
00:00:39.630 --> 00:00:43.505
Now that chatbots have large
context windows, adding a document

14
00:00:43.545 --> 00:00:47.728
is a quick way to guide their predictions
from generic into something more useful.

15
00:00:48.367 --> 00:00:49.254
Try this.

16
00:00:49.294 --> 00:00:53.578
Pick a task from your own work, such as
suggesting formative assessment ideas.

17
00:00:53.982 --> 00:00:57.383
Try it in two fresh chats,
one as a plain request,

18
00:00:57.512 --> 00:01:01.080
and once with relevant documents attached,
such as the course syllabus

19
00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:02.740
and an article you trust.

20
00:01:03.143 --> 00:01:04.343
Compare the answers.

21
00:01:05.850 --> 00:01:10.090
You’ll find a link to further reading
and all the tips so far in the post.
